{
  "id": "037-saccadic-masking",
  "title": "You can't see your own eyes move",
  "format": "short",
  "theme": "body",
  "voice": "af_heart",
  "speed": 1.0,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Look in a mirror and try to watch your own eyes move. You can't.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "eye"
        ],
        "label": "try it in a mirror"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "can't."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Look from your left eye to your right eye. Someone watching you sees your eyes move. You see nothing.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "split",
        "left": "they see movement",
        "right": "you see nothing",
        "leftIcon": "person",
        "rightIcon": "eye"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "nothing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "That's because your brain blocks your vision during every quick eye movement.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "brain"
        ],
        "label": "vision switched off"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "blocks"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It's called saccadic masking.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "saccadic",
          "masking"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "saccadic"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Without it, the world would blur every time you glanced at something.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "blur"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "blur"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It was first described in 1898.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "timeline",
        "points": [
          {
            "year": "1898",
            "label": "first described"
          },
          {
            "year": "NOW",
            "label": "still happening"
          }
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "1898."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Your brain then stitches the gaps together, so your vision feels smooth.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "gear"
        ],
        "label": "stitched seamlessly"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "stitches"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "So many times every minute, you're blind for a split second, and you never notice.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "question",
        "label": "did you notice?"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "blind"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "claim": "Saccadic masking: the brain suppresses vision during saccades; first described by Erdmann and Dodge in 1898; you cannot see your own eyes move in a mirror",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saccadic_masking"
    }
  ],
  "publish": {
    "title": "You Can't See Your Own Eyes Move 🪞",
    "description": "Try it in a mirror. Your brain switches off your vision during every eye movement. It's called saccadic masking.",
